Q: How would you read the data from the Excel file and store it into a database using Java? A: <p>My implementation approach would be:</p>
<p>Read the Excel file → Convert each row into a DTO/Entity → Validate the data → Batch insert into the database.</p>
<p>I’ll explain this using Spring Boot and Spring Data JPA.</p>
<p>For around 1,000 records, using Apache POI with Spring Data JPA’s saveAll() is sufficient.</p>
<p>To support both .xls and .xlsx files, I would use WorkbookFactory.create(file) instead of XSSFWorkbook, since WorkbookFactory automatically detects the file format and creates the appropriate Workbook implementation.</p>
<ol>
<li>Dependency</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.apache.poi&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;poi-ooxml&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;5.4.1&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
</code></pre>
<ol start="2">
<li>Entity</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Entity
public class Employee {
    @Id
    private Long empId;
    private String name;
    private String email;
    private Double salary;
    protected Employee() {
    }
    public Employee(Long empId, String name, String email, Double salary) {
        this.empId = empId;
        this.name = name;
        this.email = email;
        this.salary = salary;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<ol start="3">
<li>Repository</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public interface EmployeeRepository extends JpaRepository&lt;Employee, Long&gt; {
}
</code></pre>
<ol start="4">
<li>Read the Excel File and Store the Data in the Database</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class EmployeeExcelService {
    private final EmployeeRepository employeeRepository;
    @Transactional
    public void importEmployees(File file) throws IOException {
        List&lt;Employee&gt; employees = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        try (Workbook workbook = WorkbookFactory.create(file)) {
            Sheet sheet = workbook.getSheetAt(0);
            // Assume the first row is the header.
            for (int i = 1; i &lt;= sheet.getLastRowNum(); i++) {
                Row row = sheet.getRow(i);
                if (row == null) {
                    continue;
                }
                Long empId = (long) row.getCell(0).getNumericCellValue();
                String name = row.getCell(1).getStringCellValue();
                String email = row.getCell(2).getStringCellValue();
                Double salary = row.getCell(3).getNumericCellValue();
                Employee employee = new Employee(empId, name, email, salary);
                employees.add(employee);
            }
        }
        employeeRepository.saveAll(employees);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Overall Workflow:</p>
<pre><code>Upload/Read Excel File
        ↓
Create Workbook
        ↓
Select Sheet
        ↓
Iterate Through Rows
        ↓
Extract Cell Values
        ↓
Create Employee Entity
        ↓
Add to List
        ↓
Save All Records to the Database
</code></pre>
<p>For approximately 1,000 records, this approach is more than sufficient.</p>
<p>However, when dealing with hundreds of thousands or millions of records, I would consider using batch processing, chunk-based inserts, and streaming Excel parsing to reduce memory consumption and improve performance.</p>
